// **The only charge in the game.** Turns the contested building
// material into power — burn your bamboo or build with it — and its
// smoke raises provocation, which is what stops it being free.
//
// It used to be the dirty fallback beside a sail deck, and the sails
// were cut at M6 (`SYSTEMS.md` §5.11 open question 0): sun-versus-shade
// asked the player to read a number off the sky, and the same fork now
// chooses between danger, salvage and ground richness instead. What is
// left is one lever with a visible cost, which is the readable version
// of the choice the sails were reaching for.
#![enable(implicit_some)]
RoomDef(
    id: "room.burner",
    name: "Burner",
    short: "BRN",
    // **Gated behind `room.cutter_arm`** — there is no fuel until something cuts it. The opening
    // ladder is farm, cutter arm, burner, and then the whole
    // menu (`SYSTEMS.md` §6.11).
    unlocked_by: "room.cutter_arm",
    category: Energy,
    width: 2,
    // **A chimney, so it goes up.** The renderer already vents its smoke
    // from the roof above its column — a burner buried under six floors
    // was always fictionally odd, and the plume drawn leaving the top of
    // a tower it was nowhere near was the tell.
    //
    // The mechanical reason is that the chain needed a direction. Intake
    // is pinned to floors 0-1 because it reaches the ground, nothing was
    // pinned high, and so a whole chain could sit beside its own intake
    // and never haul anything upward. A tower that never hauls up has no
    // use for a shaft, whatever the shaft costs.
    //
    // Floor 2 rather than the roof: the garden already claims the top
    // floor, and a second claimant there would be a rule rather than a
    // decision. Two is enough to put the burner above the bamboo that
    // feeds it.
    //
    // **One, not two, since M6 cut the opening tower to two floors.**
    // What this constant is for is a *direction* — intake is pinned to
    // the ground because it reaches the ground, so something has to be
    // pinned above it or a whole chain sits beside its own intake and
    // never hauls upward. Floor 1 says that at the new scale.
    min_floor: 1,
    build_cost: [CostEntryDef(item: "item.poles", amount: 5)],
    burner: BurnerDef(
        fuel: "item.bamboo",
        // **800 charge a stalk, and the ratio is the whole balance.**
        //
        // It was 50 (2 bamboo for 100 charge) while the sails carried
        // the tower and a burner was the switch you threw on a dark
        // night. Three corrections since, each measured, each too
        // small:
        //
        // - **50** as the only income priced power at 74% of
        //   everything the tower could cut. The mill completed *zero*
        //   crafts in 1,800 ticks; every stalk went up the chimney,
        //   because `find_destination` feeds the emptiest inbox and a
        //   burner that can never be filled is always the emptiest.
        // - **200** still spent 45% (22 stalks of 49 by tick 7,200):
        //   the opening tower's real draw is ~0.61 charge/tick once
        //   striding and lamps are counted, not the 0.28 a bare
        //   arithmetic gives.
        // - **400** spent ~30%, which reads as a reasonable tax and is
        //   not. Measured against the same script on the sail economy,
        //   the mill lost **31% of its crafts** (65 against 94 over
        //   24,000 ticks) at identical harvest and with both towers
        //   striding the whole window; the golden recorder could no
        //   longer finish its shopping list inside a journey, with the
        //   comb and the ropery alone eating 60,000 of the tower's
        //   ~74,000 walking ticks.
        //
        // At 800 the bill is 6 stalks of 101, and the mill recovers to
        // 80 crafts against the sail tower's 94 — the rest of that gap
        // is buffer occupancy rather than fuel.
        //
        // **The principle behind the size of it**: the sails made
        // burning a *choice*, and a choice is allowed to be expensive.
        // As the only source it is a tax, and a tax that takes a third
        // of the economy is not a tax, it is the game. What stays
        // expensive is running the tower *hard* — a burner idles on a
        // full bank, so the fuel bill tracks striding, lamps and
        // forges rather than the clock.
        //
        // `provocation_per_burn` moves with this, always. The two are
        // one constant in two columns and tuning either alone quietly
        // retunes the siege.
        fuel_per_burn: 1,
        charge_per_burn: 800,
        // Unchanged, and now it matters more: a burner idles on a full
        // bank (`systems/power.rs`), so the duty cycle is set by what
        // the tower spends rather than by the clock. A base tower runs
        // about one burn in fourteen.
        burn_ticks: 100,
    ),
)
